'''Andrew Steven Biggs''' (born November 7, 1958)<ref>{{cite web |title= Andrew Steven Biggs |url=https://www.legistorm.com/person/bio/189182/Andrew_Steven_Biggs.html |language=en |access-date=March 29, 2021 }}</ref> is an American politician and lawyer serving as the [[United States House of Representatives|U.S. representative]] for {{ushr|AZ|5}} since 2017. A member of the [[Republican Party (United States)|Republican Party]], Biggs served as a member of the [[Arizona House of Representatives]] from 2003 to 2011 and a member of the [[Arizona Senate]] from 2011 to 2017.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.ourcampaigns.com/RaceDetail.html?RaceID=48144 |title=AZ State House 22 |date=November 6, 2002 |website=Our Campaigns |access-date=September 11, 2019 |archive-date=December 9, 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201209024235/https://www.ourcampaigns.com/RaceDetail.html?RaceID=48144 |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.ourcampaigns.com/RaceDetail.html?RaceID=323399 |title=AZ State House 22 |date=November 4, 2006 |website=Our Campaigns |access-date=September 11, 2019 |archive-date=December 23, 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20191223180736/https://www.ourcampaigns.com/RaceDetail.html?RaceID=323399 |url-status=live }}</ref> He was president of the Arizona Senate from 2013 to 2017. Biggs served as chairman of the [[Freedom Caucus]] from 2019 to 2022.<ref name=":3" />

The district, which was once represented by [[U.S. Senators]] [[John McCain]] and [[Jeff Flake]], is in the heart of the [[East Valley (Phoenix metropolitan area)|East Valley]] and includes part of [[Mesa, Arizona|Mesa]], most of [[Chandler, Arizona|Chandler]], all of [[Queen Creek, Arizona|Queen Creek]] and Biggs's residence of [[Gilbert, Arizona|Gilbert]].

Biggs is the Republican nominee in the  [[2026 Arizona gubernatorial election]]. He is challenging Governor [[Katie Hobbs]] in the general election.<ref name=nomination>{{cite news |last1=Cooper |first1=Jonathan J.|last2= Billeaudb | first2=Jacques |last3=Yu |first3=Wufei |title=Andy Biggs wins Republican nomination to challenge Democrat Katie Hobbs for Arizona governor |url=https://apnews.com/article/arizona-primary-election-biggs-schweikert-hobbs-6ecadbc05dc0704285d1f9fbfc3af0cb |access-date=July 22, 2026 |publisher=[[AP News]] |date= July 22, 2026}}</ref>

==Early life==
Biggs was born on November 7, 1958, in [[Tucson, Arizona|Tucson]], [[Arizona]].<ref name=":0" /> When he was young, Biggs went on a [[Christian mission|mission]] to [[Japan]] for [[the Church of Jesus Christ of Latter-day Saints]] and learned to speak fluent [[Japanese language|Japanese]].<ref name="WGA">{{cite web|url=https://www.wga.com/magazine/2013/02/01/legislator-profile-andy-biggs-president-arizona-state-senate|title=Legislator Profile Andy Biggs, President of Arizona State Senate|publisher=[[Western Growers Association]]|date=February 1, 2013|access-date=June 17, 2021|archive-date=October 20, 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201020034715/https://www.wga.com/magazine/2013/02/01/legislator-profile-andy-biggs-president-arizona-state-senate|url-status=live}}</ref> He later earned his [[Bachelor of Arts]] in [[Asian studies]] from [[Brigham Young University]] in 1982, his [[Juris Doctor]] from the [[University of Arizona]] in 1984, and his [[Master of Arts]] in [[political science]] from [[Arizona State University]] in 1999.<ref name=":0">{{Cite web|title=Biggs, Andy|url=https://bioguide.congress.gov/search/bio/B001302|access-date=2021-01-13|website=[[Biographical Directory of the United States Congress]]|language=en|archive-date=January 14, 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210114144951/https://bioguide.congress.gov/search/bio/B001302|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|last=Barry|first=Jason|title=AZ Senate president is former $10M sweepstakes winner|url=https://www.azfamily.com/archives/az-senate-president-is-former-10m-sweepstakes-winner/article_9b881891-7083-53af-a423-a347b1193831.html|access-date=2021-02-12|website=AZFamily|language=en|archive-date=January 27, 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210127094500/https://www.azfamily.com/archives/az-senate-president-is-former-10m-sweepstakes-winner/article_9b881891-7083-53af-a423-a347b1193831.html|url-status=live}}</ref>

Biggs worked as a lawyer for a firm based in [[Hobbs, New Mexico|Hobbs]], [[New Mexico]], before relocating to [[Phoenix, Arizona|Phoenix]], where he worked as a prosecutor.<ref name="WGA" /> In 1993, he won $10 million in the [[American Family Publishers]] sweepstakes.<ref name="Barry">{{Cite news|url=http://www.azfamily.com/story/31706957/az-state-senate-president-is-former-10-million-sweepstakes-winner|title=AZ Senate president is former $10M sweepstakes winner|last=Barry|first=Jason|website=www.azfamily.com|date=April 12, 2016|access-date=June 20, 2016|archive-date=April 21, 2016|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160421134116/http://www.azfamily.com/story/31706957/az-state-senate-president-is-former-10-million-sweepstakes-winner|url-status=live}}</ref> He appeared in a [[television advertisement|TV ad]] with [[Dick Clark]] and [[Ed McMahon]] to promote the sweepstakes.<ref name="Barry"/><ref name=":2">{{Cite web|last=Lemons|first=Stephen|date=2011-02-24|title=Andy Biggs, the $10 Million Chairman, and His Tuesday Night Massacre|url=https://www.phoenixnewtimes.com/news/andy-biggs-the-10-million-chairman-and-his-tuesday-night-massacre-6503036|access-date=2021-01-21|website=Phoenix New Times|archive-date=January 28, 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210128194503/https://www.phoenixnewtimes.com/news/andy-biggs-the-10-million-chairman-and-his-tuesday-night-massacre-6503036|url-status=live}}</ref>

==Arizona State Legislature==

=== State House of Representatives ===
In 2002, with Republican Representative [[Eddie Farnsworth]] redistricted from District 30, Biggs ran in the five-way September 10 Republican primary, placing second with 5,778 votes.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.azsos.gov/election/2002/Primary/Canvass2002PE.pdf |title=State of Arizona Official Canvass 2002 Primary Election - September 10, 2002 |publisher=Secretary of State of Arizona |location=Phoenix, Arizona |page=12 |access-date=January 6, 2014 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20041026113534/http://www.azsos.gov/election/2002/Primary/Canvass2002PE.pdf |archive-date=October 26, 2004 }}</ref> Biggs and Farnsworth were unopposed in the general election, where Biggs took the first seat with 31,812 votes and Farnsworth took the second.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.azsos.gov/election/2002/General/Canvass2002GE.pdf |title=State of Arizona Official Canvass 2002 General Election - November 5, 2002 |publisher=Secretary of State of Arizona |location=Phoenix, Arizona |page=9 |access-date=January 6, 2014 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20041026113339/http://www.azsos.gov/election/2002/General/Canvass2002GE.pdf |archive-date=October 26, 2004 }}</ref>

In 2004, Biggs and Farnsworth were unopposed in the September 7 Republican primary; Farnsworth placed first and Biggs placed second with 11,202 votes.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.azsos.gov/election/2004/Primary/Canvass2004PE.pdf |title=State of Arizona Official Canvass 2004 Primary Election - September 7, 2004 |publisher=Secretary of State of Arizona |location=Phoenix, Arizona |page=10 |access-date=January 6, 2014 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20041022135941/http://www.azsos.gov/election/2004/Primary/Canvass2004PE.pdf |archive-date=October 22, 2004 }}</ref> In the three-way general election, Farnsworth took the first seat and Biggs the second with 51,932 votes, ahead of [[Libertarian Party of Arizona|Libertarian]] candidate Wade Reynolds.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.azsos.gov/election/2004/General/Canvass2004General.pdf |title=State of Arizona Official Canvass 2004 General Election - November 2, 2004 |publisher=Secretary of State of Arizona |location=Phoenix, Arizona |page=10 |access-date=January 6, 2014 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20041126091050/http://www.azsos.gov/election/2004/General/Canvass2004General.pdf |archive-date=November 26, 2004 }}</ref>

In 2006, Biggs and Farnsworth were challenged in the four-way September 12 Republican primary; Farnsworth placed first and Biggs placed second with 7,793 votes.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.azsos.gov/election/2006/Primary/Canvass2006PE.pdf |title=State of Arizona Official Canvass 2006 Primary Election - September 12, 2006 |publisher=Secretary of State of Arizona |location=Phoenix, Arizona |page=11 |access-date=January 6, 2014 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20060926060630/http://www.azsos.gov/election/2006/Primary/Canvass2006PE.pdf |archive-date=September 26, 2006 }}</ref> In the three-way general election, Farnsworth took the first seat and Biggs the second with 38,085 votes, ahead of Libertarian candidate Edward Schwebel.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.azsos.gov/election/2006/General/Canvass2006GE.pdf |title=State of Arizona Official Canvass 2006 General Election - November 7, 2006 |publisher=Secretary of State of Arizona |location=Phoenix, Arizona |page=9 |access-date=January 6, 2014 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20061206201428/http://www.azsos.gov/election/2006/General/Canvass2006GE.pdf |archive-date=December 6, 2006 }}</ref>

In 2008, with Farnsworth running for Arizona Senate and leaving a House District 22 seat open, Biggs ran in the four-way September 2 Republican primary, placing first with 9,800 votes.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.azsos.gov/election/2008/Primary/Canvass2008PE.pdf |title=State of Arizona Official Canvass 2008 Primary Election - September 2, 2008 |publisher=Secretary of State of Arizona |location=Phoenix, Arizona |page=10 |access-date=January 6, 2014 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20090424062550/http://www.azsos.gov/election/2008/Primary/Canvass2008PE.pdf |archive-date=April 24, 2009 }}</ref> Biggs and fellow Republican nominee [[Laurin Hendrix]] won the general election, where Biggs took the first seat with 59,615 votes and Hendrix the second, ahead of Democratic nominee Glenn Ray,<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.azsos.gov/election/2008/General/Canvass2008GE.pdf |title=State of Arizona Official Canvass 2008 General Election - November 4, 2008 |publisher=Secretary of State of Arizona |location=Phoenix, Arizona |page=11 |access-date=January 6, 2014 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20081219172036/http://www.azsos.gov/election/2008/General/Canvass2008GE.pdf |archive-date=December 19, 2008 }}</ref> who had run for the district's senate seat in 2006.

=== State Senate ===
[[File:Michele Bachmann with lawmakers (6254645675).jpg|thumb|The then State Senator Biggs at a press conference held by then Congresswoman Michele Bachmann|left]]In 2010, when Republican Senator [[Thayer Verschoor]] ran for [[State Treasurer of Arizona]] and left the Senate District 22 seat open, Biggs was unopposed in both the August 24 Republican primary, winning with 25,792 votes,<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.azsos.gov/election/2010/Primary/Canvass2010PE.pdf |title=State of Arizona Official Canvass 2010 Primary Election - August 24, 2010 |publisher=Secretary of State of Arizona |location=Phoenix, Arizona |page=7 |access-date=January 6, 2014 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20130920214102/http://azsos.gov/election/2010/Primary/Canvass2010PE.pdf |archive-date=September 20, 2013 }}</ref> and the November 2 general election, winning with 59,933 votes.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.azsos.gov/election/2010/General/Canvass2010GE.pdf |title=State of Arizona Official Canvass 2010 General Election - November 2, 2010 |publisher=Secretary of State of Arizona |location=Phoenix, Arizona |page=5 |access-date=January 6, 2014 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20130920230737/http://azsos.gov/election/2010/General/Canvass2010GE.pdf |archive-date=September 20, 2013 }}</ref>

In 2012, redistricted to District 12, and with incumbent Republican Senator [[John B. Nelson]] redistricted to District 13, Biggs was unopposed in both the August 28 Republican primary, winning with 19,844 votes,<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.azsos.gov/election/2012/Primary/Canvass.pdf |title=State of Arizona Official Canvass 2012 Primary Election August 28, 2012 |publisher=[[Secretary of State of Arizona]] |location=Phoenix, Arizona |page=5 |access-date=January 6, 2014 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20131112230133/http://www.azsos.gov/election/2012/Primary/Canvass.pdf |archive-date=November 12, 2013 }}</ref> and the November 6 general election, winning with 63,812 votes.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.azsos.gov/election/2012/General/Canvass2012GE.pdf |title=State of Arizona Official Canvass 2012 General Election November 6, 2012 |publisher=Secretary of State of Arizona |location=Phoenix, Arizona |page=7 |access-date=January 6, 2014 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20121224125542/http://www.azsos.gov/election/2012/General/Canvass2012GE.pdf |archive-date=December 24, 2012 }}</ref>

==U.S. House of Representatives==
[[File:Andy Biggs, official portrait, 115th Congress.jpg|thumb|Biggs portrait during 115th Congress]]
=== Elections ===
In 2016, Biggs ran for Congress from the 5th District to replace retiring Representative [[Matt Salmon]]. He led the field in the four-way Republican primary, finishing nine votes ahead of his nearest opponent, businesswoman [[Christine Jones (businesswoman)|Christine Jones]]. A recanvass boosted Biggs's margin to 16 votes, and an automatic recount confirmed him as the nominee by 27 votes.<ref>{{cite web|date=September 16, 2016|title=Christine Jones concedes after losing by 27 votes to Biggs in GOP Congress primary|url=http://www.bizjournals.com/phoenix/news/2016/09/16/christine-jones-concedes-after-losing-by-27-votes.html|access-date=May 5, 2017|publisher=Phoenix Business Journal|archive-date=May 5, 2017|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170505080953/http://www.bizjournals.com/phoenix/news/2016/09/16/christine-jones-concedes-after-losing-by-27-votes.html|url-status=live}}</ref> His primary victory virtually assured him of being the next representative from the district; the 5th and its predecessors have been in Republican hands for all but one term since 1953.{{cn|date=May 2025}}

Biggs defeated [[Democratic Party (United States)|Democratic]] nominee Talia Fuentes, 64.1% to 35.9%.<ref>{{cite web|title=Arizona's 5th Congressional District election, 2016|website=Ballotpedia |url=https://ballotpedia.org/Arizona%27s_5th_Congressional_District_election,_2016|access-date=May 5, 2017|archive-date=May 4, 2017|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170504093551/https://ballotpedia.org/Arizona%27s_5th_Congressional_District_election,_2016|url-status=live}}</ref> He was not required to give up his state senate seat under Arizona's [[resign-to-run law]]s, since he was in the last year of what would have been his final term in the chamber.{{Citation needed|date=October 2022}}

=== Tenure ===
[[File:Andy Biggs portrait (118th Congress).jpg|thumb|Biggs portrait during 118th Congress]]
Biggs is a member of the [[Congressional Western Caucus]]<ref name=":4">{{cite web|title=Members|url=https://westerncaucus.house.gov/about/membership.htm|publisher=Congressional Western Caucus|access-date=June 27, 2018|archive-date=May 2, 2019|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190502203316/https://westerncaucus.house.gov/about/membership.htm|url-status=live}}</ref> and the [[Republican Study Committee]].<ref name=":5">{{cite web|title=Member List|url=https://rsc-walker.house.gov/|access-date=November 6, 2017|archive-date=January 1, 2019|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190101195017/https://rsc-walker.house.gov/|url-status=dead}}</ref> In September 2019, he replaced [[Mark Meadows (North Carolina politician)|Mark Meadows]] as chair of the [[Freedom Caucus]].<ref name=":3">{{cite web|url=https://thehill.com/homenews/house/460808-meadows-to-be-replaced-by-biggs-as-freedom-caucus-leader|title=Meadows to be replaced by Biggs as Freedom Caucus leader|first=Juliegrace|last=Brufke|newspaper=The Hill|date=September 10, 2019|access-date=September 11, 2019|archive-date=March 11, 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210311224906/https://thehill.com/homenews/house/460808-meadows-to-be-replaced-by-biggs-as-freedom-caucus-leader|url-status=live}}</ref>

Biggs voted for the [[Tax Cuts and Jobs Act of 2017]].<ref name="Almukhtar122117">{{cite web|last1=Almukhtar|first1=Sarah|title=How Each House Member Voted on the Tax Bill|url=https://www.nytimes.com/interactive/2017/12/19/us/politics/tax-bill-house-live-vote.html|website=The New York Times|access-date=December 21, 2017|date=December 19, 2017|archive-date=December 22, 2017|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20171222053322/https://www.nytimes.com/interactive/2017/12/19/us/politics/tax-bill-house-live-vote.html?_r=0|url-status=live}}</ref> After the vote, he said the bill would "provide much-needed economic relief" to American citizens and businesses.<ref name="Sunnucks">{{cite web|last1=Sunnucks|first1=Mike|title=House passes Trump tax cuts; Arizona delegation splits on party lines|url=https://www.bizjournals.com/phoenix/news/2017/12/19/house-passes-trump-tax-cuts-arizona-delegation.html|website=Phoenix Business Journal|access-date=December 21, 2017|archive-date=March 11, 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210311224921/https://www.bizjournals.com/phoenix/news/2017/12/19/house-passes-trump-tax-cuts-arizona-delegation.html|url-status=live}}</ref>

On March 4, 2020, [[Ken Buck]] and Biggs were the only two representatives to vote against an [[Coronavirus Preparedness and Response Supplemental Appropriations Act, 2020|$8.3 billion emergency aid package]] meant to help the [[U.S. federal government response to the COVID-19 pandemic|U.S. respond to the COVID-19 pandemic]].<ref name="nyt-house-coronavirus-2020-03">{{cite web |last1=Cochrane |first1=Emily |title=House Passes $8.3 Billion Emergency Coronavirus Response Bill |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2020/03/04/us/politics/coronavirus-emergency-aid-congress.html |website=The New York Times |access-date=March 4, 2020 |date=March 4, 2020 |archive-date=March 4, 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200304185223/https://www.nytimes.com/2020/03/04/us/politics/coronavirus-emergency-aid-congress.html |url-status=live }}</ref><ref name="rc-shutt">{{cite web |last1=Shutt |first1=Jennifer |title=House OKs $8.3 billion coronavirus aid package with little debate |url=https://www.rollcall.com/2020/03/04/trump-expected-to-receive-8-3b-coronavirus-bill-from-congress-quickly/ |website=Roll Call |access-date=March 4, 2020 |date=March 4, 2020 |archive-date=March 5, 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200305003607/https://www.rollcall.com/2020/03/04/trump-expected-to-receive-8-3b-coronavirus-bill-from-congress-quickly/ |url-status=live }}</ref> In a statement, Biggs called the bill "larded-up" and "bloated."<ref name="biggs-statement-coronavirus">{{cite web |last1=Biggs |first1=Andy |title=Congressman Biggs' Statement on Coronavirus Funding |url=https://biggs.house.gov/media/press-releases/congressman-biggs-statement-coronavirus-funding |website=Andy Biggs, U.S. House of Representatives |access-date=March 4, 2020 |date=March 4, 2020 |archive-date=March 6, 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200306002538/https://biggs.house.gov/media/press-releases/congressman-biggs-statement-coronavirus-funding |url-status=live }}</ref> Ten days later, he voted against the larger [[Families First Coronavirus Response Act|Coronavirus Response Act]], which passed the House, 363–40.<ref name=":6">{{cite web|url=https://www.nytimes.com/interactive/2020/03/14/us/politics/coronavirus-house-vote.html|title=How Every House Member Voted on the Coronavirus Relief Bill|last=Lee|first=Jasmine|date=March 14, 2020|access-date=October 18, 2020|website=The New York Times|archive-date=October 3, 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201003115519/https://www.nytimes.com/interactive/2020/03/14/us/politics/coronavirus-house-vote.html|url-status=live}}</ref> Biggs helped lead the congressional resistance to federal COVID-19 pandemic aid as one of a handful of lawmakers who publicly opposed all four coronavirus relief packages passed in early 2020. Biggs said "the cure is proving worse than the disease" and asked "how much longer the American people will acquiesce to unconstitutional and crushing government action."<ref>{{cite news |last1=Stevens |first1=Allison |title=Most U.S. House Republicans back pricey COVID-19 aid. Not Andy Biggs |url=https://azmirror.com/2020/04/24/most-u-s-house-republicans-back-pricey-covid-19-aid-not-andy-biggs/ |access-date=9 May 2025 |work=Arizona Mirror |date=24 April 2020}}</ref>

====Contesting the 2020 presidential election====
[[File:Andy Biggs (49563841473).jpg|thumb|Biggs at a Trump Rally during the 2020 presidential election]]
In 2020, Biggs joined Representative [[Paul Gosar]] in a video claiming there was [[Attempts to overturn the 2020 United States presidential election|widespread voter fraud in the 2020 presidential election]]. They claimed that Arizona's voting machines were faulty, and Biggs claimed that poll watchers were allowed to participate in vote tabulations in [[Detroit]]. They also demanded an audit of [[Maricopa County, Arizona|Maricopa County]]'s vote count.<ref name="Hansen">{{cite news |last1=Hansen |first1=Ronald J. |date=17 November 2020 |title=FACT CHECK: Reps. Andy Biggs, Paul Gosar still touting election-fraud claims |work=[[The Arizona Republic]] |url=https://www.azcentral.com/story/news/politics/fact-check/2020/11/17/andy-biggs-paul-gosar-still-touting-election-fraud-claims/6334872002/ |url-status=live |access-date=20 November 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220714234946/https://www.azcentral.com/story/news/politics/fact-check/2020/11/17/andy-biggs-paul-gosar-still-touting-baseless-election-fraud-claims/6334872002/ |archive-date=July 14, 2022}}</ref> Later, Biggs claimed that 10,000 Maricopa County voters were "disenfranchised" without giving evidence.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Dana |first1=Joe |title=US Rep. Andy Biggs falsely claims 10,000 voters were 'disenfranchised' |url=https://www.12news.com/article/news/politics/elections/congressman-andy-biggs-falsely-claims-10000-voters-disenfranchised/75-07c30754-b9c4-4fe9-9ef2-04e803b47ace |access-date=20 November 2020 |work=12news |date=13 November 2020 |archive-date=November 19, 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201119094339/https://www.12news.com/article/news/politics/elections/congressman-andy-biggs-falsely-claims-10000-voters-disenfranchised/75-07c30754-b9c4-4fe9-9ef2-04e803b47ace |url-status=live }}</ref>

In December 2020, Biggs was among 126 House Republicans to sign an amicus brief for ''[[Texas v. Pennsylvania]]'', an unsuccessful lawsuit that asked the [[Supreme Court of the United States|Supreme Court]] to overturn election results from [[Georgia (U.S. state)|Georgia]], [[Michigan]], [[Pennsylvania]], and [[Wisconsin]], thereby denying [[Joe Biden]] from taking office as president.<ref>{{cite web |title=Here Are The Names Of 126 Members Of The House Who Refuse To Accept That Biden Won |url=https://www.buzzfeednews.com/article/skbaer/list-republican-house-members-overturn-election |website=BuzzFeed News |access-date=12 December 2020 |date=11 December 2020 |archive-date=March 2, 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210302024541/https://www.buzzfeednews.com/article/skbaer/list-republican-house-members-overturn-election |url-status=live }}</ref><ref name="TXvPAdocket">{{cite web |title=Motion for Leave to File Brief Amicus Curiae |url=https://www.supremecourt.gov/DocketPDF/22/22O155/163550/20201211132250339_Texas%20v.%20Pennsylvania%20Amicus%20Brief%20of%20126%20Representatives%20--%20corrected.pdf |website=SupremeCourt.gov |access-date=12 December 2020 |date=10 December 2020 |archive-date=February 23, 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210223234925/https://www.supremecourt.gov/DocketPDF/22/22O155/163550/20201211132250339_Texas%20v.%20Pennsylvania%20Amicus%20Brief%20of%20126%20Representatives%20--%20corrected.pdf |url-status=live }}</ref>

Biggs also spoke at rallies promoting the "[[Attempts to overturn the 2020 United States presidential election#Stop the Steal|Stop the Steal]]" election conspiracy movement, and has claimed [[Antifa (United States)|antifa]] was behind the January 6, 2021, [[storming of the U.S. Capitol]].<ref name="Anglen">{{cite news |last1=Anglen |first1=Robert |last2=Hansen |first2=Ronald J. |title=New videos emerge tying Andy Biggs to 'Stop the Steal,' but he's pointing to antifa |url=https://eu.azcentral.com/story/news/local/arizona-investigations/2021/01/13/biggs-points-finger-antifa-new-videos-show-his-ties-stop-steal/6645979002/ |access-date=29 January 2021 |work=The Arizona Republic |archive-date=July 14, 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220714234946/https://www.azcentral.com/story/news/local/arizona-investigations/2021/01/13/biggs-points-finger-antifa-new-videos-show-his-ties-stop-steal/6645979002/ |url-status=live }}</ref> He denied that he was involved in planning the event and the riots as alleged by ''[[Rolling Stone]]'' and a video posted by Stop the Steal organizer [[Ali Alexander]].<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.abc15.com/news/region-phoenix-metro/central-phoenix/arizona-congressmen-deny-rolling-stones-report-they-helped-plan-jan-6-insurrection|title=Arizona congressmen deny 'Rolling Stones' report they helped plan Jan. 6 insurrection|date=October 26, 2021|website=KNXV|access-date=October 27, 2021|archive-date=November 9, 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211109072734/https://www.abc15.com/news/region-phoenix-metro/central-phoenix/arizona-congressmen-deny-rolling-stones-report-they-helped-plan-jan-6-insurrection|url-status=live}}</ref> In a deposition to the January 6 committee of the House of Representatives, Alexander testified that he spoke in person with Biggs before the events.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.politico.com/news/2021/12/18/stop-the-steal-founder-jan-6-committee-gop-lawmakers-525345|title = 'Stop the Steal' founder told Jan. 6 committee about contacts with GOP lawmakers|website = [[Politico]]| date=December 18, 2021 |access-date = March 7, 2022|archive-date = March 7, 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220307075957/https://www.politico.com/news/2021/12/18/stop-the-steal-founder-jan-6-committee-gop-lawmakers-525345|url-status = live}}</ref>

On October 7, 2021, Biggs again falsely claimed that "we don't know who won Arizona in the 2020 presidential election."<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.nbcnews.com/politics/elections/house-democrats-seek-answers-hearing-arizona-election-review-n1280973|title=GOP Rep. Biggs falsely claims 'we don't know' who won Arizona in hearing on partisan ballot review|website=NBC News|date=October 7, 2021 |access-date=November 4, 2021|archive-date=November 2, 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211102203445/https://www.nbcnews.com/politics/elections/house-democrats-seek-answers-hearing-arizona-election-review-n1280973|url-status=live}}</ref>

====2021 attack on the United States Capitol====
During the [[2021 United States Capitol attack|2021 attack on the U.S. Capitol]], Biggs and all other House members were ushered to a secure location when the House chamber was cleared. A video of Biggs later surfaced in which he [[Face masks during the COVID-19 pandemic in the United States|refused to wear a face mask during the Capitol attack]], a violation of House rules at the time.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.cnn.com/2021/01/09/politics/republican-congress-members-refuse-masks-trnd/index.html|title=Republican members of Congress refuse to wear masks during Capitol insurrection|author=Keri Enriquez|website=CNN|date=January 9, 2021|access-date=January 11, 2021|archive-date=February 16, 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210216143853/https://www.cnn.com/2021/01/09/politics/republican-congress-members-refuse-masks-trnd/index.html|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/politics/2021/01/10/lawmakers-may-have-been-exposed-coronavirus-capitol-lockdown-attending-physician-says/|title=Lawmakers may have been exposed to the coronavirus in Capitol lockdown, attending physician says|first1=Paulina|last1=Firozi|first2=Amy B.|last2=Wang|first3=Mike|last3=DeBonis|via=www.washingtonpost.com|access-date=January 11, 2021|archive-date=January 13, 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210113194511/https://www.washingtonpost.com/politics/2021/01/10/lawmakers-may-have-been-exposed-coronavirus-capitol-lockdown-attending-physician-says/|url-status=live}}</ref> Biggs subsequently [[2021 United States Electoral College vote count#Joint session of Congress|voted to object]] to Arizona's and Pennsylvania's electoral votes that day, joining [[Sedition Caucus|138 other House Republicans]].<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.nytimes.com/interactive/2021/01/07/us/elections/electoral-college-biden-objectors.html|title=The 147 Republicans Who Voted to Overturn Election Results|first1=Karen|last1=Yourish|first2=Larry|last2=Buchanan|first3=Denise|last3=Lu|work=[[The New York Times]]|date=January 7, 2021|access-date=June 17, 2021|archive-date=January 9, 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210109000730/https://www.nytimes.com/interactive/2021/01/07/us/elections/electoral-college-biden-objectors.html|url-status=live}}</ref>

On January 12, 2021, Biggs called on Representative [[Liz Cheney]] to resign from her leadership position in the Republican caucus after she voted in favor of [[Donald Trump]]'s [[Second impeachment of Donald Trump|second impeachment]].<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.washingtonexaminer.com/news/politics/freedom-caucus-liz-cheney-resign-impeachment|title=House Freedom Caucus chairman calls on Cheney to resign from leadership over impeachment support|date=January 13, 2021|website=Washington Examiner|access-date=January 18, 2021|archive-date=January 15, 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210115043228/https://www.washingtonexaminer.com/news/politics/freedom-caucus-liz-cheney-resign-impeachment|url-status=live}}</ref>

In the aftermath of the events on January 6, Biggs's brothers William and Daniel wrote a letter to the editor of ''[[The Arizona Republic]]'' demanding their brother's removal from office. They wrote that Biggs is "at least partially to blame" for the Capitol storming. They also condemned his refusal to wear a mask in the secure location.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.azcentral.com/story/news/politics/arizona/2021/01/20/rep-andy-biggs-brothers-andy-spread-election-fraud-lies/4235799001/|title=Rep. Andy Biggs' brothers: 'Andy spread election fraud lies'|first=Ronald J.|last=Hansen|website=The Arizona Republic|access-date=January 22, 2021|archive-date=July 14, 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220714234946/https://www.azcentral.com/story/news/politics/arizona/2021/01/20/rep-andy-biggs-brothers-andy-spread-election-fraud-lies/4235799001/|url-status=live}}</ref>

Biggs was one of 12 House Republicans to vote against H.R 1085 to award three [[Congressional Gold Medal]]s to the [[U.S. Capitol Police]] who protected the Capitol on January 6.<ref>{{Cite web|date=17 March 2021|title=Final Vote Results For Roll Call 87|url=https://clerk.house.gov/evs/2021/roll087.xml|url-status=live|website=United States House of Representatives|access-date=March 18, 2021|archive-date=March 18, 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210318000110/https://clerk.house.gov/evs/2021/roll087.xml}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news|last=Itkowitz|first=Colby|title=A dozen Republicans voted against Congressional Gold Medals for police who protected them on Jan. 6|language=en-US|newspaper=Washington Post|url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/politics/2021/03/17/dozen-republicans-voted-against-congressional-gold-medals-police-who-protected-them-jan-6/|access-date=2021-03-18|issn=0190-8286|archive-date=March 18, 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210318021806/https://www.washingtonpost.com/politics/2021/03/17/dozen-republicans-voted-against-congressional-gold-medals-police-who-protected-them-jan-6/|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|last=|first=|date=2021-03-16|title=H.R.1085 - 117th Congress (2021-2022): To award three congressional gold medals to the United States Capitol Police and those who protected the U.S. Capitol on January 6, 2021.|url=https://www.congress.gov/bill/117th-congress/house-bill/1085|url-status=live|access-date=2021-03-18|website=www.congress.gov|archive-date=March 18, 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210318021923/https://www.congress.gov/bill/117th-congress/house-bill/1085}}</ref> In June 2021, he and 20 other House Republicans voted against a similar resolution.<ref name=twsCNN99078>{{cite news|first1=Annie|last1=Grayer|first2=Kristin|last2=Wilson|date=June 16, 2021|publisher=CNN|url=https://www.cnn.com/2021/06/15/politics/congressional-gold-medal-house-vote/index.html|title=21 Republicans vote no on bill to award Congressional Gold Medal for January 6 police officers|access-date=June 16, 2021|archive-date=June 28, 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210628154838/https://www.cnn.com/2021/06/15/politics/congressional-gold-medal-house-vote/index.html|url-status=live}}</ref>

The [[United States House Select Committee on the January 6 Attack|House committee investigating the January 6 attack]] subpoenaed Biggs on May 12, 2022.<ref>{{cite news|title=January 6 committee subpoenas 5 GOP lawmakers close to Trump, including McCarthy|url=https://www.cbsnews.com/news/january-6-committee-subpoenas-kevin-mccarthy-jim-jordan-mo-brooks-scott-perry-andy-biggs/|publisher=CBS News|date=May 12, 2022|author1=Scott MacFarlane|author2=Melissa Quinn|author3=Kathryn Watson|access-date=May 13, 2022|archive-date=May 13, 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220513110616/https://www.cbsnews.com/news/january-6-committee-subpoenas-kevin-mccarthy-jim-jordan-mo-brooks-scott-perry-andy-biggs/|url-status=live}}</ref> During the committee hearing on June 21, 2022, Speaker of the Arizona House [[Russell Bowers]] testified that Biggs called him on the morning of January 6 asking him to sign a letter calling for decertification of the Arizona electors.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.axios.com/2022/06/21/rusty-bowers-andy-biggs-arizona-trump | title=Arizona House speaker Rusty Bowers said Rep. Biggs pressed him on decertifying electors | newspaper=Axios | date=June 21, 2022 | last1=Solender | first1=Andrew | access-date=June 21, 2022 | archive-date=June 22, 2022 | archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220622003631/https://www.axios.com/2022/06/21/rusty-bowers-andy-biggs-arizona-trump | url-status=live }}</ref> On June 23, 2022, witnesses confirmed that Biggs had asked for a presidential pardon for his activities related to the certification of the vote.<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://january6th.house.gov/ |title=Select Committee to Investigate the January 6th Attack on the United States Capitol &#124; |access-date=July 14, 2022 |archive-date=November 10, 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211110062814/https://january6th.house.gov/ |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Rojas |first=Warren |date=June 23, 2022 |title=At least 6 GOP members of Congress asked the Trump White House for a January 6 pardon, including Matt Gaetz and Marjorie Taylor Greene |url=https://www.businessinsider.com/cheney-to-reveal-gop-republican-colleagues-sought-jan-6-pardons-2022-6 |access-date=2022-07-12 |website=Business Insider |language=en-US |archive-date=June 24, 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220624112527/https://www.businessinsider.com/cheney-to-reveal-gop-republican-colleagues-sought-jan-6-pardons-2022-6 |url-status=live }}</ref>

====Foreign and defense policy====
Biggs was among 60 Republicans to oppose condemning [[2019 Turkish offensive into north-eastern Syria|Trump's action of withdrawing forces from Syria]].<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://projects.propublica.org/represent/votes/116/house/1/560|title=Opposes Withdrawal of U.S. Forces in... - H.J.RES.77: Opposing the decision to end certain United...|first=Derek|last=Willis|website=ProPublica|date=August 12, 2015|access-date=April 5, 2021|archive-date=December 7, 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201207221532/https://projects.propublica.org/represent/votes/116/house/1/560|url-status=live}}</ref> He, [[Matt Gaetz]], and a handful of other Republicans broke with their party and voted to end Saudi assistance in relation to the [[Saudi Arabian-led intervention in Yemen|War in Yemen]].<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.theamericanconservative.com/articles/the-brave-cadre-of-conservative-anti-war-republicans/|title=The Small But Brave Cadre of Conservative Anti-War Republicans|website=The American Conservative|date=November 19, 2019|access-date=April 5, 2021|archive-date=February 28, 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210228095401/https://www.theamericanconservative.com/articles/the-brave-cadre-of-conservative-anti-war-republicans/|url-status=live}}</ref>

On March 19, 2021, Biggs voted against a House resolution to condemn the [[2021 Myanmar coup d'état|military coup]] in [[Myanmar]]. The resolution passed, 398–14, with one other member, [[Paul Gosar]], voting present. Biggs called the violence "tragic" but added that "there is suffering everywhere in the world" and the U.S. "can't be the military police for the entire world," saying the resolution was a way to "put our foot in the door in Burma."<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.forbes.com/sites/andrewsolender/2021/03/19/14-house-republicans-vote-against-condemning-myanmar-military-coup/|title=14 House Republicans Vote Against Condemning Myanmar Military Coup|first=Andrew|last=Solender|website=Forbes|access-date=March 20, 2021|archive-date=March 21, 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210321134819/https://www.forbes.com/sites/andrewsolender/2021/03/19/14-house-republicans-vote-against-condemning-myanmar-military-coup/|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|url=https://thehill.com/homenews/house/544075-14-republicans-vote-against-resolution-condemning-military-coup-in-myanmar|title=14 Republicans vote against resolution condemning Myanmar military coup|first=Tal|last=Axelrod|date=March 19, 2021|website=The Hill|access-date=March 20, 2021|archive-date=March 21, 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210321134858/https://thehill.com/homenews/house/544075-14-republicans-vote-against-resolution-condemning-military-coup-in-myanmar|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.cnn.com/2021/03/19/politics/house-republicans-myanmar-coup/index.html|title=14 House Republicans vote against a measure condemning military coup in Myanmar|publisher=CNN|last1=Diaz|first1=Daniella|last2=Wilson|first2=Kristin|date=March 19, 2021|access-date=March 24, 2021|archive-date=March 21, 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210321170915/https://www.cnn.com/2021/03/19/politics/house-republicans-myanmar-coup/index.html|url-status=live}}</ref> The resolution was symbolic and did not call for use of force.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.phoenixnewtimes.com/news/congressman-andy-biggs-votes-against-condemning-genocide-in-myanmar-9935499|title=Why Did Andy Biggs Vote Against Condemning Genocide in Myanmar?|first=Antonia Noori|last=Farzan|date=December 7, 2017|website=Phoenix New Times|access-date=March 22, 2021|archive-date=January 15, 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210115051905/https://www.phoenixnewtimes.com/news/congressman-andy-biggs-votes-against-condemning-genocide-in-myanmar-9935499|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|last=Levin|first=Andy|date=2021-03-19|title=Text - H.Res.134 - 117th Congress (2021-2022)|url=https://www.congress.gov/bill/117th-congress/house-resolution/134/text|url-status=live|access-date=2021-03-27|website=www.congress.gov|archive-date=March 21, 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210321160129/https://www.congress.gov/bill/117th-congress/house-resolution/134/text}}</ref>

In June 2021, Biggs was one of 49 House Republicans to vote to repeal the [[Authorization for Use of Military Force Against Iraq Resolution of 2002]].<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.nbcnews.com/politics/congress/house-set-repeal-2002-iraq-war-authorization-n1271107|title = House votes to repeal 2002 Iraq War authorization|website = [[NBC News]]| date=June 17, 2021 |access-date = June 17, 2021|archive-date = June 18, 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210618123910/https://www.nbcnews.com/politics/congress/house-set-repeal-2002-iraq-war-authorization-n1271107|url-status = live}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |title=FINAL VOTE RESULTS FOR ROLL CALL 172| publisher=Office of the Clerk, U.S. House of Representatives |url=https://clerk.house.gov/evs/2021/roll172.xml|url-status=live|date=June 17, 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210627182635/https://clerk.house.gov/evs/2021/roll172.xml |archive-date=June 27, 2021}}</ref>

In July 2021, Biggs voted against the bipartisan ALLIES Act, which would increase by 8,000 the number of [[Special Immigrant Visa|special immigrant visas]] for Afghan allies of the U.S. military during [[United States invasion of Afghanistan|its invasion of Afghanistan]], while also reducing some application requirements that caused long application backlogs; the bill passed the House, 407–16.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Quarshie |first1=Mabinty |title=These 16 Republicans voted against speeding up visas for Afghans fleeing the Taliban |url=https://www.usatoday.com/story/news/politics/2021/08/17/16-republicans-voted-against-special-visas-help-afghanistan-people/8163392002/ |access-date=August 18, 2021 |work=[[USA Today]] |date=August 17, 2021 |archive-date=August 17, 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210817221148/https://www.usatoday.com/story/news/politics/2021/08/17/16-republicans-voted-against-special-visas-help-afghanistan-people/8163392002/ |url-status=live }}</ref>

In September 2021, Biggs was among 75 House Republicans to vote against the National Defense Authorization Act of 2022, which contains a provision that would require women to be drafted.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://thehill.com/policy/defense/573751-house-passes-sweeping-defense-policy-bill|title=House passes sweeping defense policy bill|first=Regina|last=Zilbermints|date=September 23, 2021|website=TheHill|access-date=September 24, 2021|archive-date=September 25, 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210925230121/https://thehill.com/policy/defense/573751-house-passes-sweeping-defense-policy-bill|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.govtrack.us/congress/votes/117-2021/h293|title=H.R. 4350: National Defense Authorization Act for Fiscal Year 2022 -- House Vote #293 -- Sep 23, 2021|website=GovTrack.us|access-date=September 24, 2021|archive-date=September 24, 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210924151443/https://www.govtrack.us/congress/votes/117-2021/h293|url-status=live}}</ref>

In 2022, Biggs voted against a bill to provide approximately $14 billion to the government of Ukraine.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.axios.com/house-funding-bill-ukraine-637ec64d-6d77-492e-85dc-231f90b284c7.html|title=Congress passes $1.5 trillion bill to fund government|date=March 11, 2022|work=Axios|first=Andrew|last=Solender|access-date=March 23, 2022}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|url=https://projects.propublica.org/represent/votes/117/house/2/66|title = On Concurring in Senate Amdt with... - H.R.2471: To measure the progress of post-disaster|date = August 12, 2015|website=projects.propublica.org|access-date=March 23, 2022}}</ref>

In July 2022, Biggs was one of 18 Republicans to vote against ratifying Sweden's and Finland's applications for NATO membership.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://thehill.com/homenews/house/3565022-the-18-house-republicans-who-voted-against-a-resolution-to-support-finland-sweden-joining-nato/ | title=The 18 House Republicans who voted against a resolution to support Finland, Sweden joining NATO|work=The Hill|first=Mychael|last=Schnell|date=July 19, 2022|access-date=May 28, 2024}}</ref>

In 2023, Biggs was among 47 Republicans to vote in favor of H.Con.Res. 21, which directed President Biden to remove U.S. troops from [[Syria]] within 180 days.<ref>{{cite web | title=H.Con.Res. 21: Directing the President, pursuant to section 5(c) of … -- House Vote #136 -- Mar 8, 2023 | website=GovTrack.us | date=8 March 2023 |url=https://www.govtrack.us/congress/votes/118-2023/h136 | access-date=3 April 2023}}</ref><ref>{{cite web | title=House Votes Down Bill Directing Removal of Troops From Syria | website=US News & World Report | date=8 March 2023 |url=http://www.usnews.com/news/politics/articles/2023-03-08/house-votes-down-bill-directing-removal-of-troops-from-syria | access-date=3 April 2023}}</ref>

On March 19, 2024, Biggs voted NAY to House Resolution 149, which condemned the illegal abduction and forcible transfer of children from Ukraine to the Russian Federation. He was one of nine Republicans to do so.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.businessinsider.com/house-republicans-vote-against-condemning-russian-abduction-ukrainian-children-2024-3 |first1=Bryan |last1=Metzger |date=2024-03-19 |website=businessinsider.com |title=These 9 House Republicans voted against a resolution condemning the Russian abduction of Ukrainian children}}</ref>

====2023 Speaker election====
{{Wikinews|118th United States Congress convenes; House of Representatives adjourns without electing Speaker for first time in 100 years}}
Biggs ran in the Republican conference election for [[Speaker of the United States House of Representatives|Speaker of the House of Representatives]] against [[Kevin McCarthy]], then the [[House Minority Leader]]. McCarthy defeated him, 188 votes to 31.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Zanona |first1=Melanie |last2= Rogers |first2=Alex |last3=Raju |first3=Manu |title=Kevin McCarthy beats far-right challenger 188-31 to lead House GOP |url=https://edition.cnn.com/2022/11/15/politics/house-republican-vote-kevin-mccarthy/index.html |access-date=3 January 2023 |work=CNN |date=15 November 2022 |language=en}}</ref> In the [[January 2023 Speaker of the United States House of Representatives election|2023 Speaker election]], fellow [[Freedom Caucus]] member [[Paul Gosar]] nominated Biggs.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Wong |first1=Scott |last2=Talbot |first2=Henry |title=McCarthy's speaker bid in peril after Republican defections deny him majority |url=https://www.nbcnews.com/politics/congress/mccarthys-bid-house-speaker-remains-uncertain-moment-truth-arrives-rcna61553 |access-date=3 January 2023 |work=NBC News |date=3 January 2023 |language=en}}</ref> He received 10 votes on the first ballot, which, alongside nine votes for other Republican candidates, was enough to necessitate a second ballot.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Walsh |first1=Deirdre |last2=Jones |first2=Dustin |title=McCarthy loses first vote for House speaker, forcing a second voting round |url=https://www.npr.org/2023/01/03/1146600160/mccarthy-scrambles-for-votes-to-be-elected-speaker-of-the-house |access-date=3 January 2023 |work=NPR |date=3 January 2023 |language=en}}</ref> This made the 2023 election the first to take more than one ballot since [[1923 Speaker of the United States House of Representatives election|1923]].<ref>{{cite news |last1=Doherty |first1=Erin |title=How the House speaker vote will work |url=https://www.axios.com/2023/01/03/house-speaker-vote-mccarthy |access-date=3 January 2023 |work=Axios |date=3 January 2023 |language=en}}</ref> Biggs was not nominated for the second ballot, and voted for [[Jim Jordan]]. He did not receive any votes on ballots 2 through 13, but received two votes on the 14th ballot despite not being nominated.

==== Removal of Kevin McCarthy as Speaker of the House ====
On October 3, 2023, Biggs was one of eight Republicans who voted [[Removal of Kevin McCarthy as Speaker of the House|to remove Kevin McCarthy]] as Speaker of the House; during the debate which preceded the vote, Biggs had criticized McCarthy for allowing the passage of a [[2024 United States federal budget#September 2023 continuing resolution|temporary spending bill]] which did not include provisions to enhance [[border security]].{{cn|date=May 2025}}

