==Political positions==

Biggs previously chaired the [[Freedom Caucus]], which has been described as [[right-wing populist]].<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.theatlantic.com/politics/archive/2017/04/in-the-freedom-caucus-trump-meets-his-match/522243/|title=In The Freedom Caucus, Trump Meets His Match|first=Michelle|last=Cottle|date=April 7, 2017|website=The Atlantic|access-date=April 6, 2021|archive-date=June 24, 2019|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190624192006/https://www.theatlantic.com/politics/archive/2017/04/in-the-freedom-caucus-trump-meets-his-match/522243/|url-status=live}}</ref>

=== Abortion ===
Biggs is "opposed to all forms of [[elective abortion]]."<ref name="Fischer62716">{{cite news|first=Howard|last=Fischer|date= February 18, 2020|orig-date=original date June 27, 2016| title=Supreme Court ruling could invalidate Arizona abortion rules |newspaper=Arizona Daily Sun |url=https://azdailysun.com/news/local/supreme-court-ruling-could-invalidate-arizona-abortion-rules/article_b4dfc842-63c7-560b-94e4-8bdae2bb392a.html|archive-date=December 22, 2017|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20171222052623/http://azdailysun.com/news/local/supreme-court-ruling-could-invalidate-arizona-abortion-rules/article_b4dfc842-63c7-560b-94e4-8bdae2bb392a.html|url-status=live}}</ref> He has argued in favor of abolishing the [[Filibuster in the United States Senate|filibuster]] to make it easier to pass anti-abortion laws.<ref>{{Cite news|url=http://ktar.com/story/2077577/rep-andy-biggs-change-senate-rules-that-hinder-gop-pro-life-agenda/|title=Rep. Andy Biggs: Change Senate rules that hinder GOP pro-life agenda|date=May 23, 2018|work=KTAR.com|access-date=October 22, 2018|language=en-US|archive-date=October 23, 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20181023034806/http://ktar.com/story/2077577/rep-andy-biggs-change-senate-rules-that-hinder-gop-pro-life-agenda/|url-status=live}}</ref> He supported the 2022 [[Dobbs v. Jackson Women's Health Organization|overturning of ''Roe v. Wade'']], calling it "a major victory for the unborn."<ref>{{cite web |last1=Biggs |first1=Andy |title=Today's Supreme Court decision to overturn Roe v. Wade is a major victory for the unborn. LIFE WINS. |url=https://twitter.com/RepAndyBiggsAZ |website=Twitter |access-date=25 June 2022 |language=en |date=24 June 2022 |archive-date=June 24, 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220624133623/https://twitter.com/RepAndyBiggsAZ |url-status=live }}</ref>

===Agriculture===
Biggs opposes [[federal preemption]] of state and local laws regulating agricultural products sold across state lines, including those related to [[animal welfare]]. In March 2024, Biggs was among ten House Republicans who signed a letter to the [[United States House Committee on Agriculture|House Committee on Agriculture]] opposing the inclusion of the Ending Agricultural Trade Suppression (EATS) Act in the 2024 [[United States farm bill|farm bill]].<ref>{{cite web |last1=Clayton |first1=Chris |title=GOP Conservatives Oppose EATS Act Over Chinese Influence in US Pork Industry |url=https://www.dtnpf.com/agriculture/web/ag/news/article/2024/03/08/gop-conservatives-oppose-eats-act-us |website=Progressive Farmer |publisher=DTN |access-date=23 May 2025 |date=8 March 2024}}</ref> The EATS Act sought to invalidate certain state laws establishing minimum space requirements for animal products sold within the state, such as [[2018 California Proposition 12|California's Proposition 12]]. The letter argued that the legislation would undermine [[states' rights]] and harm U.S. national security, while increasing the influence of foreign-owned corporations, especially those based in China, over the U.S. agricultural sector.<ref>{{cite web |title=Rep. Anna Paulina Luna Leads in Letter Fighting Against Foreign Control of the U.S. Pork Industry |url=https://luna.house.gov/posts/icymi-rep-anna-paulina-luna-leads-in-letter-fighting-against-foreign-control-of-the-u-s-pork-industry |access-date=23 May 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240414004611/https://luna.house.gov/posts/icymi-rep-anna-paulina-luna-leads-in-letter-fighting-against-foreign-control-of-the-u-s-pork-industry |archive-date=14 April 2024 |date=8 March 2024}}</ref>

===Article V convention===
Biggs opposes a [[convention to propose amendments to the United States Constitution]], also known as an Article V convention.<ref>{{cite news |last=Hansen |first=Ronald J. |date=February 19, 2021 |title=Rep. Andy Biggs has long history on conservatism's fringes |url=https://www.azcentral.com/story/news/politics/arizona/2021/02/19/rep-andy-biggs-has-long-history-conservatisms-fringes/6740694002/ |work=[[The Arizona Republic]] |access-date=March 8, 2022 }}</ref> During his tenure as Arizona Senate president, Biggs blocked a resolution calling for a convention.<ref>{{cite news |last=Beard Rau |first=Alia |date=September 9, 2017 |title=For first time in 150 years, state lawmakers will gather to plan constitutional convention |url=https://www.azcentral.com/story/news/politics/legislature/2017/09/09/article-v-constitutional-convention-planners-convene-in-arizona/618218001/ |work=[[The Arizona Republic]] |access-date=March 8, 2022 |archive-date=March 10, 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220310014821/https://www.azcentral.com/story/news/politics/legislature/2017/09/09/article-v-constitutional-convention-planners-convene-in-arizona/618218001/ |url-status=live }}</ref> In 2015, Biggs published a book, ''The Con of the Con-Con,'' arguing against a convention.<ref>{{cite news |last=Giles |first=Ben |date=May 12, 2015 |title=In 155 pages, Biggs fights back against conservative critics |url=https://azcapitoltimes.com/news/2015/05/12/in-155-pages-biggs-fights-back-against-conservative-critics/ |work=[[Arizona Capitol Times]] |access-date=March 8, 2022 |archive-date=March 8, 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220308202600/https://azcapitoltimes.com/news/2015/05/12/in-155-pages-biggs-fights-back-against-conservative-critics/ |url-status=live }}</ref>

=== Climate change ===
Biggs rejects the [[Scientific opinion on climate change|scientific consensus on climate change]].<ref>{{cite news |title=Rep. Andy Biggs jeered on health care, climate change at Mesa town hall |first=Ronald J. |last=Hansen |newspaper=[[The Arizona Republic]] |date=April 11, 2017 |access-date=August 29, 2017 |url=https://www.azcentral.com/story/news/politics/arizona/2017/04/12/andy-biggs-town-hall-arizona/100124436/ |archive-date=July 14, 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220714234946/https://www.azcentral.com/story/news/politics/arizona/2017/04/12/andy-biggs-town-hall-arizona/100124436/ |url-status=live }}</ref> Replying to a candidate survey from ''The Arizona Republic'', Biggs wrote, "I do not believe climate change is occurring. I do not think that humans have a significant impact on climate. The federal government should stop regulating and stomping on our economy and freedoms in the name of a discredited theory."<ref>{{cite news |magazine=[[Scientific American]] |title=House Science Panel Adds Climate-Denying Members |first=Scott |last=Waldman |date=January 17, 2017 |access-date=March 20, 2020 |url=https://www.scientificamerican.com/article/house-science-panel-adds-climate-denying-members/ |agency=[[Environment & Energy Publishing]] |archive-date=August 30, 2017 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170830012548/https://www.scientificamerican.com/article/house-science-panel-adds-climate-denying-members/ |url-status=live }}</ref><ref name=newrepublic>{{cite news |first=Emily |last=Atkin |magazine=[[The New Republic]] |title=Minutes |url=https://newrepublic.com/minutes/142035/climate-denying-congressman-compared-einstein-constituents-booed-him |access-date=March 20, 2020 |date=August 29, 2017 |archive-date=March 20, 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200320171906/https://newrepublic.com/minutes/142035/climate-denying-congressman-compared-einstein-constituents-booed-him |url-status=live }}</ref><ref name=axios>{{cite news |agency=[[Axios (website)|Axios]] |url=https://www.axios.com/house-republican-introduces-measure-to-defund-key-climate-research-2479158452.html |first=Shane |last=Savitsky |title=House Republican introduces measure to defund key climate research |access-date=March 20, 2020 |date=August 29, 2017 |archive-date=March 20, 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200320171905/https://www.axios.com/house-republican-introduces-measure-to-defund-key-climate-research-2479158452.html |url-status=live }}</ref> He submitted an amendment to the 2018 spending bill that would defund the [[National Climate Assessment]]<ref name="axios" /> and urged President Trump to withdraw from the [[Paris Agreement|Paris Accords]].<ref>{{cite news |title=Biggs Urges Trump To Withdraw From Paris Climate Agreement, Flake Pushing NAFTA |agency=Arizona Daily Independent |url=https://arizonadailyindependent.com/2017/06/01/biggs-urges-trump-to-withdraw-from-paris-climate-agreement/ |date=June 1, 2017 |access-date=March 20, 2020 |archive-date=March 20, 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200320171906/https://arizonadailyindependent.com/2017/06/01/biggs-urges-trump-to-withdraw-from-paris-climate-agreement/ |url-status=live }}</ref> In February 2020, when Republican House [[Minority Leader]] [[Kevin McCarthy]] attempted to make a modest effort to gather the support of concerned young voters via a restrained approach to [[climate change mitigation|address climate change]], Biggs and other hardline denialists objected. Biggs said: "People are like, 'Is this an official rollout? It can't be official. We didn't vote on it'."<ref name=AGW/>

While factions of the Republican Party were split on whether to continue [[climate change denial]],<ref name="AGW" /> conservative groups such as the [[Club for Growth]] and the [[Competitive Enterprise Institute]] supported continuation.<ref name="AGW">{{cite news| last1=Zanona | first1=Melanie | last2=Adragna | first2=Anthony | last3=Wolff | first3=Eric | title=Kevin McCarthy faces uneasy right flank over climate push |publisher=[[Politico]]| date=13 February 2020 |url=https://www.politico.com/news/2020/02/13/gop-climate-change-kevin-mccarthy-115025|url-status=live|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210226112710/https://www.politico.com/news/2020/02/13/gop-climate-change-kevin-mccarthy-115025 |archive-date=February 26, 2021}}</ref><ref>[https://www.energyandpolicy.org/americans-for-prosperity-anti-wind-letter-analysis/ Analysis: Americans for Prosperity Anti-Wind Letter] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200702203831/https://www.energyandpolicy.org/americans-for-prosperity-anti-wind-letter-analysis/ |date=July 2, 2020 }}, ''Energy and Policy Institute,  June 2014. Retrieved March 20, 2020.''</ref> In 2018, Biggs was the sole House member to receive a 100% rating from the CFG.<ref>{{cite news|last1=Persons|first1=Sally|title=Rep. Andy Biggs, lone House member, gets top score on Club for Growth's annual economic scorecard|url=https://www.washingtontimes.com/news/2018/feb/22/andy-biggs-lone-house-member-gets-top-score-on-clu/|access-date=March 20, 2020|work=Washington Times|date=February 22, 2018|archive-date=March 20, 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200320235411/https://www.washingtontimes.com/news/2018/feb/22/andy-biggs-lone-house-member-gets-top-score-on-clu/|url-status=live}}</ref>

===COVID-19===
{{see also|COVID-19 pandemic in Arizona}}
Biggs opposes wearing masks to prevent the transmission of COVID-19, encouraging Arizonans not to wear them.<ref name="Innes">{{cite news |last1=Innes |first1=Stephanie |title=Arizona Congressman Andy Biggs tweets against wearing masks, gives other questionable COVID-19 advice |url=https://www.azcentral.com/story/news/local/arizona-health/2020/09/08/rep-andy-biggs-tweets-covid-19-wearing-masks-hydroxychloroquine/5748715002/ |access-date=20 November 2020 |work=The Arizona Republic |date=8 September 2020 |archive-date=March 11, 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210311225728/https://www.azcentral.com/story/news/local/arizona-health/2020/09/08/rep-andy-biggs-tweets-covid-19-wearing-masks-hydroxychloroquine/5748715002/ |url-status=live }}</ref> In July 2020, he tweeted that people should not trust [[Anthony Fauci]] or [[Deborah Birx]].<ref name=krejci >{{cite web |last1=Krejci |first1=Cleo |title=Contradicting medical evidence and health experts, Biggs, Gosar cast doubt on severity of COVID-19 in Arizona |url=https://www.azcentral.com/story/news/politics/arizona/2020/07/02/andy-biggs-paul-gosar-cast-doubt-severity-covid-19-arizona/3258469001/ |website=The Arizona Republic |date=2 July 2020|archive-date=March 11, 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210311225647/https://www.azcentral.com/story/news/politics/arizona/2020/07/02/andy-biggs-paul-gosar-cast-doubt-severity-covid-19-arizona/3258469001/ |url-status=live }}</ref> He has called for the [[White House Coronavirus Task Force]] to be disbanded.<ref>{{cite web |last1=Fernandez |first1=Marisa |title=GOP congressman calls for coronavirus task force to be disbanded so Trump "is not undermined" |url=https://www.axios.com/andy-biggs-coronavirus-task-force-trump-1389f3f4-d443-460e-8233-8a162b74eb21.html |website=Axios |date=July 2, 2020 |access-date=18 May 2021 |language=en |archive-date=March 25, 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210325182832/https://www.axios.com/andy-biggs-coronavirus-task-force-trump-1389f3f4-d443-460e-8233-8a162b74eb21.html |url-status=live }}</ref> During a major outbreak in the summer of 2020 in Arizona, Biggs questioned the hospitalization numbers and called Governor [[Doug Ducey]]'s two-month lockdown a result of "hysteria" from "Democratic Leftists."<ref name=krejci/> In September 2020, Biggs posted a series of tweets supporting the use of [[hydroxychloroquine]] to prevent COVID-19.<ref name="Innes"/>

===Healthcare===
In 2018, Biggs sponsored a bill "designed to let very sick patients request access to experimental medicines without government oversight," which passed the House, 267–149. Biggs said the bill was "not false hope; it is hope."<ref>{{cite web|last1=Karlin-Smith|first1=Sarah|title=House passes right-to-try on second try|url=https://www.politico.com/story/2018/03/21/drugs-right-to-try-congress-434677|website=Politico|date=March 21, 2018 |access-date=March 22, 2018|archive-date=March 22, 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180322031614/https://www.politico.com/story/2018/03/21/drugs-right-to-try-congress-434677|url-status=live}}</ref>

===Juneteenth===
In June 2021, Biggs was among 14 House Republicans who voted against passing legislation to establish June 19, or [[Juneteenth]], as a federal holiday.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.cnn.com/2021/06/16/politics/house-vote-juneteenth-federal-holiday-senate-passed-june-19/index.html|title=Congress passes bill making Juneteenth a federal holiday|publisher=CNN|last1=Grayer|first1=Annie|last2=Diaz|first2=Danielle|date=June 16, 2021|access-date=June 16, 2021|archive-date=June 18, 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210618100712/https://www.cnn.com/2021/06/16/politics/house-vote-juneteenth-federal-holiday-senate-passed-june-19/index.html|url-status=live}}</ref>

=== LGBTQ rights ===
Biggs is a former policy advisor to [[United Families International]], a nonprofit that opposes [[same-sex marriage]].<ref name="Hendley3912">{{cite web|last1=Hendley|first1=Matthew|title=Andy Biggs, Other Politicos Tied to Gilbert Religious Group Labeled as Anti-Gay "Hate Group" by Southern Poverty Law Center|url=http://www.phoenixnewtimes.com/news/andy-biggs-other-politicos-tied-to-gilbert-religious-group-labeled-as-anti-gay-hate-group-by-southern-poverty-law-center-6654888|website=Phoenix New Times|access-date=December 21, 2017|date=March 9, 2012|archive-date=December 22, 2017|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20171222052238/http://www.phoenixnewtimes.com/news/andy-biggs-other-politicos-tied-to-gilbert-religious-group-labeled-as-anti-gay-hate-group-by-southern-poverty-law-center-6654888|url-status=live}}</ref> Biggs condemned the Supreme Court ruling in ''[[Obergefell v. Hodges]]'', which held that same-sex marriage bans violated the US constitution.<ref>{{Cite web |date=March 17, 2020 |first=Lee |last=Fang |title=Anti-Gay Lawmaker Voted Against Coronavirus Bill Because It "Redefined Family" by Providing Sick Leave to Domestic Partners |url=https://theintercept.com/2020/03/17/coronavirus-stimulus-bill-andy-biggs/ |access-date=2022-04-11 |website=The Intercept |language=en |archive-date=June 5, 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200605003101/https://theintercept.com/2020/03/17/coronavirus-stimulus-bill-andy-biggs/ |url-status=live }}</ref>

=== Net neutrality ===
Biggs has gone on record as opposing [[net neutrality]], and favored [[Federal Communications Commission|FCC]] Chairman [[Ajit Pai]]'s plan to end it. In a letter to his constituents, Biggs wrote, "we should allow the free market to expand the internet and its services."<ref>{{Cite news|url=https://www.theverge.com/2017/12/11/16746230/net-neutrality-fcc-isp-congress-campaign-contribution|title=All 535 members of Congress, and how much money they got from ISPs|work=The Verge|access-date=July 11, 2018|archive-date=July 11, 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180711185422/https://www.theverge.com/2017/12/11/16746230/net-neutrality-fcc-isp-congress-campaign-contribution|url-status=live}}</ref>

===Robert Mueller===
On June 23, 2017, Biggs was one of three Republicans who called for the resignation of [[Robert Mueller]], the prosecutor investigating [[Russian interference in the 2016 United States elections|Russian interference in the 2016 presidential election]], on the grounds that Mueller could not conduct his investigation fairly because of events that happened when he was the director of the [[Federal Bureau of Investigation]].<ref name="twsNewsy">Newsy, [http://start.att.net/player/article/newsy_now-3_house_republicans_call_mueller_compromised_deman-newsy 3 House Republicans Call Mueller Compromised, Demand Resignation] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20171107022124/http://start.att.net/player/article/newsy_now-3_house_republicans_call_mueller_compromised_deman-newsy |date=November 7, 2017 }}, Retrieved November 4, 2017, "...Reps. Gaetz, Biggs and Gohmert think Mueller can't fairly conduct his Russia investigation because of events that happened while he was FBI director..."</ref>

On March 19, 2018, Biggs renewed his call for Mueller to resign.<ref name="Congressman Andy Biggs (Press Release)">{{cite web|date=March 19, 2018|title=Congressman Biggs Renews Call for Robert Mueller to Resign|url=https://biggs.house.gov/media/press-releases/congressman-biggs-renews-call-robert-mueller-resign|access-date=July 27, 2018|archive-date=July 26, 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180726033854/https://biggs.house.gov/media/press-releases/congressman-biggs-renews-call-robert-mueller-resign|url-status=live}}</ref> On July 25, 2018, Biggs and nine other Republicans co-sponsored a resolution to [[Federal impeachment in the United States|impeach]] [[United States Deputy Attorney General|Deputy Attorney General]] [[Rod Rosenstein]],<ref name="Arizona Republic">{{cite web|date=July 25, 2018|title=Biggs, Gosar join calls in U.S. House to impeach Deputy AG Rosenstein|url=https://www.azcentral.com/story/news/politics/arizona/2018/07/25/biggs-gosar-join-calls-some-house-impeach-rosenstein/838935002/|access-date=July 27, 2018|archive-date=July 14, 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220714234947/https://www.azcentral.com/story/news/politics/arizona/2018/07/25/biggs-gosar-join-calls-some-house-impeach-rosenstein/838935002/|url-status=live}}</ref> who was Mueller's direct supervisor after the recusal of [[United States Attorney General|Attorney General]] [[Jeff Sessions]].<ref name="CNN">{{cite web|date=April 11, 2018|title=Who would be Mueller's boss if Rosenstein goes?|website=[[CNN]]|url=https://www.cnn.com/2018/04/11/politics/rod-rosenstein-doj-succession-plan/index.html|access-date=July 27, 2018|archive-date=July 28, 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180728035657/https://www.cnn.com/2018/04/11/politics/rod-rosenstein-doj-succession-plan/index.html|url-status=live}}</ref>

On April 8, 2019, ''The Arizona Republic'' published an [[op-ed]] by Biggs on the initial findings of the [[Mueller special counsel investigation|Mueller investigation]].<ref>{{Cite web|title=I never flipped on the Mueller report. It supported what I said all along|url=https://www.azcentral.com/story/opinion/op-ed/2019/04/08/mueller-report-andy-biggs-consistent/3381583002/|access-date=April 19, 2019|website=azcentral|language=en|archive-date=July 14, 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220714234947/https://www.azcentral.com/story/opinion/op-ed/2019/04/08/mueller-report-andy-biggs-consistent/3381583002/|url-status=live}}</ref> In it, Biggs called the investigation "an illegitimate attack on the executive branch" and wrote that the findings "demonstrate the weakness of the initial premise to investigate Trump, his family and campaign staff." He blamed the investigation on "the media that fueled this bogus attempt to overthrow the will of the American voter." Biggs's op-ed was published well ahead of the release of Mueller's full report on April 18, 2019, and was most likely written in response to a four-page summary of the report by Attorney General [[William Barr]] released on March 24. After the publication of the full report, Biggs posted a video on Twitter declaring that there was "no basis for an [[Obstruction of justice|obstruction [of justice]]] charge" against Trump, chastising the Democratic party for attempting to "undermine the POTUS."<ref>{{Cite web|title=Mueller report: Arizona's congressional delegation reacts|url=https://www.msn.com/en-us/news/politics/mueller-report-arizonas-congressional-delegation-reacts/ar-BBW51hz|access-date=April 19, 2019|website=www.msn.com|archive-date=December 21, 2019|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20191221130828/https://www.msn.com/en-us/news/politics/mueller-report-arizonas-congressional-delegation-reacts/ar-BBW51hz|url-status=live}}</ref>

=== Texting while driving ===
In 2017, Biggs used his powers as transportation chair and president of the Arizona State Senate to block a bill banning [[driving while texting]] for holders of a learning permit.<ref>{{cite news |last=Pitzl |first=Mary Jo |date=April 5, 2017 |title=Texting ban for drivers? Arizona legislature pushing the brakes on the idea |website=The Arizona Republic |url=https://www.azcentral.com/story/news/politics/politicalinsider/2017/04/05/r-u-4-txt-ban-arizona-legislative-chairman-not-sure/100003164/ |url-status=live |access-date=March 20, 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220714234948/https://www.azcentral.com/story/news/politics/politicalinsider/2017/04/05/r-u-4-txt-ban-arizona-legislative-chairman-not-sure/100003164/ |archive-date=July 14, 2022}}</ref>

===9/11 Victims Compensation Fund===
In 2019, Biggs was one of 11 House Republicans to oppose funding for the [[September 11th Victim Compensation Fund|September 11 Victims Compensation Fund]] bill H.R. 1327.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.sourcepolitics.com/11-republicans-vote-against-helping-9-11-first-responders/|title=11 Republicans vote against helping 9/11 first responders|date=July 13, 2019|website=Source Politics|access-date=January 29, 2020|archive-date=December 24, 2019|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20191224204052/https://sourcepolitics.com/11-republicans-vote-against-helping-9-11-first-responders/|url-status=live}}</ref> On July 12, 2019, the measure passed the House, 402–12.{{cn|date=May 2025}}

===Israel===
Biggs voted to provide Israel with support following [[2023 Hamas attack on Israel]].<ref>{{Cite news |last=Demirjian |first=Karoun |date=2023-10-25 |title=House Declares Solidarity With Israel in First Legislation Under New Speaker |language=en-US |work=The New York Times |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2023/10/25/us/politics/house-israel-vote.html |access-date=2023-10-30 |issn=0362-4331}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last1=Washington |first1=U. S. Capitol Room H154 |last2=p:225-7000 |first2=DC 20515-6601 |date=2023-10-25 |title=Roll Call 528 Roll Call 528, Bill Number: H. Res. 771, 118th Congress, 1st Session |url=https://clerk.house.gov/Votes/2023528 |access-date=2023-10-30 |website=Office of the Clerk, U.S. House of Representatives |language=en}}</ref>

===Antitrust===
In 2022, Biggs was one of 39 Republicans to vote for the Merger Filing Fee Modernization Act of 2022, an antitrust package that would crack down on corporations for anti-competitive behavior.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.cnbc.com/2022/09/29/house-passes-antitrust-bill-raising-ma-fees.html | title=House passes antitrust bill that hikes M&A fees as larger efforts targeting tech have stalled | website=[[CNBC]] | date=September 29, 2022 }}</ref><ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.govtrack.us/congress/votes/117-2022/h460 | title=H.R. 3843: Merger Filing Fee Modernization Act of 2022 -- House Vote #460 -- Sep 29, 2022 }}</ref>

===2023 United States debt-ceiling crisis===
{{main|2023 United States debt-ceiling crisis}}

In April 2023, Biggs was one of four Republican representatives who voted against the proposed [[2023 United States debt-ceiling crisis#Limit, Save, Grow Act|Limit, Save, Grow Act]], which raised the debt ceiling while at the same time providing for spending cuts, saying that the proposed spending and deficit reductions contained in the bill were insufficient.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://clerk.house.gov/Votes/2023199 | title=Roll Call 199 Roll Call 199, Bill Number: H. R. 2811, 118th Congress, 1st Session | newspaper=Office of the Clerk, U.S. House of Representatives | date=April 26, 2023}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|url=https://biggs.house.gov/media/press-releases/congressman-biggs-releases-statement-house-leaderships-paltry-debt-ceiling#:~:text=The%20Limit%2C%20Save%2C%20Grow%20Act%20is%20touted%20to%20%E2%80%9Csave,growth%20of%20our%20national%20debt.|title=Congressman Biggs Releases Statement on House Leadership's Paltry Debt Ceiling Legislation|date=April 26, 2023 }}</ref>

In June of the same year, Biggs was among the 71 Republicans who voted against final passage of the [[Fiscal Responsibility Act of 2023]] in the House.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://thehill.com/homenews/house/4029522-republicans-and-democrats-who-bucked-party-leaders-by-voting-no/|title=Republicans and Democrats who bucked party leaders by voting no|first=Jared|last=Gans|date=May 31, 2023|access-date=June 6, 2023|work=[[The Hill (newspaper)|The Hill]]}}</ref>

== Personal life ==
Biggs married Cindy Biggs in 1982. They have six children and eleven grandchildren.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.azcentral.com/story/news/politics/arizona/2025/04/18/arizona-rep-andy-biggs-daughter-dies-of-cancer/83158332007/|title=Arizona Rep. Andy Biggs' daughter dies of cancer}}</ref><ref name="Hendley3912" /> One of their daughters, Cosette, died at age 37 of cancer in 2025, leaving behind a husband and three children.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Grado |first1=Gary |title=Rep. Andy Biggs' daughter dies at 37 {{!}} Arizona Capitol Times |url=https://azcapitoltimes.com/news/2025/04/18/rep-andy-biggs-daughter-dies-at-37/ |access-date=9 May 2025 |publisher=Arizona Capitol Times |date=18 April 2025}}</ref>

